---

### HIGH Severity Findings

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**Ref:** Peruma et al., tsDetect (ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 31–38)

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — if/else means one branch is never validated per run
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Fix — split into two explicit data-driven cases:**
```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow(2, "PremiumProcessed")]   // SKU-1 qty 2 → over 100
[DataRow(1, "StandardProcessed")]  // SKU-1 qty 1 → under 100
public void ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus(int qty, string expectedStatus)
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", qty) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual(expectedStatus, result.Status);
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** One code path silently goes untested on every run. Coverage looks complete but isn't.

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)
**Ref:** Peruma et al., tsDetect (ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43–48)

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — no assertion; passes even if ProcessOrder returns garbage
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// method ends
```

**Fix — assert on the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(_db.WasOrderSaved(order.Id));
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test contributes to coverage numbers but catches zero regressions.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test
**Ref:** Spadini et al., MSR 2020 (consistently rated serious by practitioners)
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 98)

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — hard-coded 2-second wait; flaky on slow CI, wasteful on fast machines
Thread.Sleep(2000);
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

**Fix — use a polling helper or make the fake synchronously observable:**
```csharp
